## Why This Is Asked

This question probes hands-on experience building streaming joins with per-key state, TTL-based invalidation, and robust delivery guarantees in KCNA-like pipelines.

## Key Concepts

- Per-sensor TTL cache
- Event-time join with watermarks
- Side-input-driven re-enrichment
- Offset tracking and at-least-once semantics

## Code Example

```java
// Pseudo KCNA sink skeleton
class CalibratedJoinSink {
  Map<String, Calibration> cache;
  void onCalibration(Calibration c){ cache.put(c.sensorId, c); }
  void onReading(Reading r){
    Calibration c = cache.get(r.sensorId);
    r.factor = (c != null) ? c.factor : 1.0;
    emit(r);
    // optionally track buffered items for late calibration
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to handle calibration TTL expiry without missed re-enrichment?
- How to test robustness under high-cardinality sensors and bursty data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-region ordering, coordination, failover recovery, and durable state management in a realistic KCNA-like system.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant global sequence
- Cross-region coordination
- Durable storage and idempotent processing
- Reordering buffers for in-order emission
- Failure/recovery paths and testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal sketch
class Coordinator {
  constructor(store) { this.store = store; }
  allocate(tenantId) {
    const last = this.store[tenantId]?.lastSeq || 0;
    const next = last + 1;
    this.store[tenantId] = { lastSeq: next };
    return next;
  }
  commit(tenantId, seq) {
    // durable commit placeholder
    this.store[tenantId].lastSeq = Math.max(this.store[tenantId].lastSeq, seq);
  }
}
class Consumer {
  constructor(coord) { this.coord = coord; this.buff = new Map(); }
  onMessage(msg) {
    const { tenantId, globalSeq } = msg.meta;
    if (!this.buff.has(tenantId)) this.buff.set(tenantId, new Map());
    const map = this.buff.get(tenantId);
    map.set(globalSeq, msg);
    this.flush(tenantId);
  }
  flush(tenantId) {
    const map = this.buff.get(tenantId);
    let seq = this.coord.peekNext(tenantId);
    while (map.has(seq)) {
      const m = map.get(seq);
      // process(m)
      map.delete(seq);
      seq++;
      this.coord.commit(tenantId, seq);
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle clock skew and coordinator failover?
- What metrics would you surface for latency and duplicates?

## Why This Is Asked

This explores privacy-preserving cross-tenant analytics, auditing, and key management in KCNA at scale, aligning with enterprise needs around data sharing without leakage.

## Key Concepts

- Differential privacy and secure aggregation
- Per-tenant access controls and key rotation
- Tamper-evident auditing of query results
- API design for aggregated analytics
- Performance considerations under multi-tenant load

## Code Example

```javascript
// Demonstrative DP aggregation stub
function clipAndNoisify(values, epsilon, sensitivity) {
  const clipped = values.map(v => Math.max(-sensitivity, Math.min(sensitivity, v)));
  const b = sensitivity / epsilon;
  const noise = (Math.random() < 0.5 ? -1 : 1) * b * Math.log(1 - Math.random());
  return clipped.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0) + noise;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure privacy budget consumption over time in a live dashboard?
- What changes would you make to support streaming DP with backpressure and multi-tenant isolation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a streaming gate with per-user policy, token verification, per-user caches, and idempotence considerations under multi-tenant load.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming token validation per user
- TTL-based per-user caches
- Exact vs at-least-once semantics in gates
- Canary rollout and rollback safety

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: gate events with per-user tokens
function processEvent(e, tokenStore) {
  const t = tokenStore.get(e.userId);
  if (!t) return {forward:false, reason:'no-token'};
  if (Date.now() >= t.expiry) return {forward:false, reason:'expired'};
  if (!verifySignature(e, t.signature)) return {forward:false, reason:'bad-signature'};
  return {forward:true};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would token revocation during peak load affect latency and correctness?
- How would you test clock skew and cross-partition consistency for token expiry?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-topic correlation with late data, per-key state, and exactly-once guarantees, plus practical testing and recovery considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Windowing and watermarking for cross-topic joins
- Per-user bounded state store
- Idempotent emission and deduplication across partitions
- Backpressure handling and rebalance safety

## Code Example
```java
// Pseudocode
Map<String, ClickInfo> lastClick = new HashMap<>();
void onClick(Click c) { lastClick.put(c.userId, new ClickInfo(c.clickTs, c.item)); }
void onPurchase(Purchase p) {
  ClickInfo c = lastClick.get(p.userId);
  if (c != null && p.purchaseTs - c.clickTs <= 5*60*1000) {
    emit(new CompositeEvent(p.userId, c.clickTs, p.purchaseTs, c.item, p.amount), p.userId + p.purchaseTs);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test window expiry, rebalances, and dedup across partitions?
- How would you adapt for extremely high churn users and late-arriving events?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design robust multi-tenant data quality controls, handles schema drift, and preserves latency SLAs under backpressure.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant schema catalogs and versioning
- QualityGate operator contracts and DLQ routing
- Backward-compatible schema evolution and drift metrics
- Latency budgeting and backpressure handling
- Canary testing with synthetic tenants

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: QualityGate interface for KCNA
class QualityGate {
  constructor(schemaCatalog){ this.catalog = schemaCatalog }
  validate(event){ const schema = this.catalog.get(event.tenantId, event.version); return schema ? schema.validate(event) : false }
  route(event, valid){ return valid ? event : { error: 'schema-violation' } }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure drift latency and false positives in production?
- How do you handle a tenant upgrading to a new schema mid-stream without dropping data?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a time-aware, multi-sink Kafka workflow with correct offset handling and idempotence when routing late-arriving data.

## Key Concepts
- EventTime versus wall-clock time
- Late-arrival detection with separate sink routing
- Global idempotence across multiple sinks
- Offset commit semantics in multi-path processing
- Canary test design for on-time versus late events

## Code Example
```javascript
function onMessage(msg) {
  if (isLate(msg.eventTime)) {
    routeToSink('late_user_events', msg)
  } else {
    routeToSink('analytics', msg)
  }
  commitOffsets([msg.offset])
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes ability to design robust, scalable schema evolution in a multi-tenant streaming system, including compatibility rules, rollouts, and testing under mixed versions.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant schema registry
- Versioned event envelopes
- Backward/forward compatibility
- Rolling upgrades and dual writes
- Data migration and validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: coerce event to current schema version per tenant
function coerce(event, currentVersion) {
  if (event.schemaVersion === currentVersion) return event;
  if (currentVersion === 2 && event.schemaVersion === 1) {
    return { ...event, region: event.region ?? "unknown", schemaVersion: 2 };
  }
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle removing a field or changing types across tenants?
- How would you instrument metrics to detect schema drift and upgrade failures?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes design for governance in KCNA, focusing on isolation, performance, and auditing in a multi-tenant streaming pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation and per-tenant quotas
- Ingestion-time enforcement and back-pressure
- Retention policies and per-tenant TTL
- Tamper-evident audits and event-based violations

## Code Example
```javascript
function checkQuota(tenantId, byteSize, policyStore, quotas) {
  const q = policyStore[tenantId] || quotas.default;
  if (q.bytesUsed + byteSize > q.bytesLimit) return false;
  q.bytesUsed += byteSize;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test privacy guarantees to ensure no cross-tenant data leakage?
- How would you handle dynamic quota changes and tenant churn?

